// A `Mask<T, N>` carries NO arithmetic operators, and enforcing that is half the reason it is a separate
// type from `Simd` rather than the `Simd<bool, N>` the design first considered. Its lanes are
// all-ones/all-zeros BIT PATTERNS, not numbers, so `m * n` is nonsense — but a mask is a raw vector type
// in C, so without a rule it lands on the primitive-operator path and compiles in silence.
//
// (The other half is that `select` lives on the mask, so it cannot be handed a data vector by mistake.)
fn int32 main() {
    Simd<float32, 4> a = [1.0f32; 4];
    Mask<float32, 4> m = a.greaterThan(rhs: a);
    Mask<float32, 4> p = m * m;
    return 0;
}
